.TH BENCHMARK\-STORAGE\-PTS 1 "June 2026" "benchmarking" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh \- benchmark storage I/O performance with fio (direct) or the Phoronix Test Suite
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh
\fB\-\-disk\fR \fI"<device>;<label>"\fR
[\fB\-\-disk\fR \fI"<device>;<label>"\fR ...]
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh
\fB\-\-disk\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B WARNING: This script is destructive.
It formats and completely wipes all data on every target disk before testing.
.PP
.B Safety.
Before any write, each target is validated and the run aborts (touching nothing)
if any target, or any of its partitions or holders, is the root/boot disk,
mounted elsewhere, an LVM physical volume, an MD RAID member, or active swap.
These guards are always enforced and are not bypassed by
.BR \-\-force .
After printing the list of disks to be wiped, destruction must be authorized: on
a terminal by typing
.BR WIPE ,
or with
.B \-\-force
(non-interactive runs without
.B \-\-force
abort). The exit cleanup only releases disks the script actually began
preparing, so an aborted run never wipes an untouched disk.
.PP
Benchmarks storage I/O across one or more disks sequentially.
For each disk the script formats it with XFS, mounts it, runs the selected
benchmark method, and saves results before moving on to the next disk.
Tests are never run in parallel across disks to avoid I/O contention.
.PP
Two methods are available, selected with \fB\-\-method\fR:
.TP
.B direct
(default) Calls
.BR fio (1)
directly with a small, curated, time-boxed workload matrix and reports IOPS,
throughput and p50/p99/p99.9 latency in a summary table, plus a CSV and one JSON
file per workload. Fast (minutes per disk) and does not require the Phoronix Test
Suite. Output goes to
.B fio\-direct\-<label>/
under the run directory.
.TP
.B pts
Legacy Phoronix Test Suite path: runs
.BR pts/fio ", " pts/dbench ", " pts/fs\-mark
(and
.B pts/compilebench
where Python\ 2 exists). Slower and reports no latency, but is the only method
that uploads to OpenBenchmarking.org (\fB\-\-upload\fR).
.PP
.B SSD steady-state preconditioning
is performed by default before formatting each SSD or NVMe device.
The script writes across the full device twice (128\ KiB blocks, queue depth\ 32)
to move it through its garbage-collection and wear-levelling cycle so that
measurements reflect steady-state performance rather than a rested state.
Preconditioning is skipped automatically for HDDs and unknown device types,
and can be disabled with \fB\-\-skip\-preconditioning\fR.
.PP
Dependencies are installed automatically on supported distributions (Ubuntu,
Debian, Rocky Linux, openSUSE): only
.BR fio (1)
and filesystem tools for the direct method, or the full Phoronix Test Suite for
.BR "\-\-method pts" .
.PP
Results are collected into
.B ./benchmark\-results/<result\-id>/
relative to the directory from which the script is invoked.
.SH DIRECT METHOD WORKLOADS
The default
.B direct
method runs these fio workloads (direct\ I/O, engine io_uring with libaio
fallback, fixed numjobs/iodepth per workload so results are comparable across
hosts):
.TP
.B randread_4k_qd1 ", " randread_4k_qd32
4\ KiB random read at queue depth\ 1 (latency floor) and\ 32 (saturated IOPS).
.TP
.B randwrite_4k_qd1 ", " randwrite_4k_qd32
4\ KiB random write at queue depth\ 1 and\ 32.
.TP
.B randrw_4k_70_30_qd32
4\ KiB 70/30 read/write mixed workload at queue depth\ 32.
.TP
.B seqread_1m_qd16 ", " seqwrite_1m_qd16
1\ MiB sequential read and write (throughput).
.PP
.B "\-\-mode quick"
runs only the four QD32/sequential workloads with shorter runtimes.
.SH PTS TESTS (\-\-method pts)
.TP
.B pts/fio
Flexible I/O tester (full PTS option sweep).
.TP
.B pts/dbench
Filesystem metadata throughput using CIFS-style workloads.
.TP
.B pts/fs\-mark
Filesystem throughput benchmark focused on file creation and deletion patterns.
.TP
.B pts/compilebench
Simulates kernel compilation I/O patterns (requires Python\ 2; skipped automatically
when only Python\ 3 is available).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Disk target options (at least one required)
.TP
.B \-\-disk " " \fI"<device>;<label>"\fR
Add a target disk.
.I device
is the block device path (e.g.\&
.BR /dev/vdb ).
.I label
is a short name used for the mount point
.RB ( /mnt/<label> )
and result directory names.
The argument must be quoted so the shell does not interpret
.RB ' ; '
as a command separator.
May be repeated for multiple disks.
.TP
.B \-\-disk\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Read disk entries from a file, one
.I device;label
entry per line.
Lines beginning with
.RB ' # '
and blank lines are ignored.
May be combined with \fB\-\-disk\fR.
.SS General options
.TP
.B \-\-force ", " \-\-yes ", " \-y
Authorize destruction without the interactive confirmation prompt.
Required to run destructively in non-interactive contexts (nohup/SSH/cron);
without it such runs abort safely.
Does
.B not
bypass the safety guards that refuse the root/boot disk, mounted filesystems,
LVM physical volumes, MD RAID members and swap (those are never wiped).
.TP
.B \-\-method " " \fIdirect|pts\fR
Benchmark method (default
.BR direct ).
See
.B BENCHMARK METHODS
above.
.TP
.B \-\-mode " " \fIquick|full\fR
Run profile for the direct method (default
.BR full ).
.B quick
runs a four-workload subset with shorter runtimes and skips preconditioning;
.B full
runs all seven workloads.
.TP
.B \-\-fio\-size " " \fISIZE\fR
Direct-method test-file size (default
.BR 8G ),
auto-capped to about 80% of free space. Larger sizes span more of the device so
random results are not flattered by controller caching. Accepts IEC suffixes
(e.g.\&
.BR 4G ", " 16G ", " 100G ).
.TP
.B \-\-fio\-engine " " \fIENGINE\fR
Direct-method fio I/O engine (default
.BR auto ,
which prefers io_uring and falls back to libaio).
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-preconditioning
Skip SSD steady-state preconditioning passes.
Use when re-running tests immediately after a previous run (the device is
already conditioned) or when turnaround time matters more than strict
reproducibility.
.TP
.B \-\-upload
Upload results to OpenBenchmarking.org after the run completes.
Requires \fB\-\-method pts\fR, plus \fB\-\-result\-id\fR and \fB\-\-result\-name\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-result\-id " " \fIID\fR
Short identifier for the run (e.g.\&
.IR ceph\-dc1\-q1\-2026 ).
Used as the output directory name and the upload identifier on
OpenBenchmarking.org.
Default: auto-generated timestamp string.
.TP
.B \-\-result\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Human-readable display name for the result (e.g.\&
.IR "Ceph DC1 \- NVMe vs HDD Q1 2026" ).
Required when \fB\-\-upload\fR is specified.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a help message and exit.
.SH DISK FILE FORMAT
One entry per line in the format
.IR device ; label .
Lines starting with
.RB ' # '
and blank lines are ignored.
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Storage benchmark disk list
/dev/vdb;NVMe_Replica3
/dev/vdc;NVMe_EC32

/dev/vdd;HDD_Replica3
/dev/vde;HDD_EC32
.RE
.fi
.SH OUTPUT
.TP
.B ./benchmark\-results/<result\-id>/<result\-id>\-system\-snapshot.txt
System snapshot captured before the run.
.TP
.BI ./benchmark\-results/<result\-id>/fio\-direct\- label /
(direct method) One directory per disk, containing
.BR summary.txt " (IOPS/throughput/p50/p99 latency table),"
.BR summary.csv " (same data incl. p99.9), and one " <workload>.json
of raw fio output per workload.
.TP
.BI ./benchmark\-results/<result\-id>/<label>_ test _result/
(\fB\-\-method pts\fR) One PTS result directory per (disk\~\[mu]\~test)
combination (e.g.\&
.BR NVMe_Replica3_fio_result/ ),
each containing
.B composite.xml
and all result data for that test on that disk.
.SH COMPARISON
The
.B direct
method writes a finished summary table per disk; it is not processed by
.BR create\-report\-pts.sh (1).
For
.BR "\-\-method pts" ,
a local comparison report is generated at the end using
.BR "phoronix\-test\-suite compare\-results" ,
and full reports across disks or runs can be produced with
.BR create\-report\-pts.sh (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Test two disks with different characteristics in a single run:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\&./benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh \\
    \-\-disk "/dev/vdb;NVMe_Replica3" \\
    \-\-disk "/dev/vdc;HDD_Replica3"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Quick direct-fio sanity check on one disk:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\&./benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh \-\-disk "/dev/vdb;nvme" \-\-mode quick \\
    \-\-result\-id "nvme\-smoke"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Legacy PTS method with upload to OpenBenchmarking.org:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\&./benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh \-\-disk\-file disks.conf \-\-method pts \-\-upload \\
    \-\-result\-name "Ceph NVMe vs HDD \- Q1 2026" \\
    \-\-result\-id "ceph\-dc1\-q1\-2026"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Skip preconditioning for a quick re-run immediately after a previous run:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\&./benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh \-\-disk\-file disks.conf \\
    \-\-skip\-preconditioning \\
    \-\-result\-id "ceph\-dc1\-q1\-2026\-rerun"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Compare two separate runs (before and after a storage upgrade) using
.BR create\-report\-pts.sh (1):
.PP
.nf
.RS
\&./benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh \-\-disk "/dev/vdb;NVMe" \-\-result\-id "before\-upgrade"
\&./benchmark\-storage\-pts.sh \-\-disk "/dev/vdb;NVMe" \-\-result\-id "after\-upgrade"
\&./create\-report\-pts.sh \\
    ./benchmark\-results/before\-upgrade/ \\
    ./benchmark\-results/after\-upgrade/
.RE
.fi
